Introduction

Large language models (LLMs) have transformed artificial intelligence. Given a sequence of tokens $x_1,\dots,x_t$, a transformer trained on next‑token prediction learns a conditional distribution $P_\theta(x_{t+1}\mid x_{\le t})$ that captures the statistical regularities of a massive corpus. The result is a system that can generate passable essays, translate languages, and even write code. Yet, after trillions of parameters and trillions of training tokens, a fundamental gap remains: the model can produce the word “apple” in appropriate contexts but knows nothing of what it is like to see, grasp, drop, or bite an apple. It does not know that an apple falls under gravity, that it can be bruised by impact, or that it will decay over time. In short, it lacks grounded semantic understanding.

This gap is not a temporary engineering limitation, but a consequence of the level of reasoning at which LLMs operate. Pearl’s Causal Hierarchy (Pearl, 2009) distinguishes three levels of cognitive capacity: Level 1 (Association) — purely observational probabilities $P(Y|X)$; Level 2 (Intervention) — probabilities under deliberate actions, $P(Y|do(X))$; and Level 3 (Counterfactuals) — reasoning about what would have happened had a different action been taken. Next‑token prediction, no matter how large the model, remains strictly at Level 1. Grounding, on the other hand, requires at minimum the ability to answer “what if?” questions—a capacity that is only possible with knowledge of the causal structure of the world.

This dissertation formalises a bold hypothesis: AGI is not the consequence of token prediction, but the consequence of causal compression of embodied experience. In mathematical terms, we define:

\text{Understanding} = \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{E}),

where $\mathcal{E}$ is the full stream of sensorimotor experience—perceptions, actions, and their consequences—and $\mathcal{C}$ is a causal compression operator that distills from that stream a compact, causal world model. The agent does not merely observe; it acts, observes the consequences of its actions, and updates its internal representation in a closed loop:

\Phi = \mathcal{M} \circ \mathcal{C} \circ \mathcal{A} \circ \mathcal{P}.

Here, $\mathcal{P}$ perceives the world, $\mathcal{A}$ selects an intervention, $\mathcal{C}$ compresses the new outcome into a refined world model, and $\mathcal{M}$ consolidates the change in memory. Repeating this loop should, if the world is sufficiently rich, drive the agent’s internal abstraction toward a unique, stable representation of the world’s causal structure.

The central theoretical challenge is to prove that this convergence actually happens and to identify the minimal conditions under which it does. This requires moving from philosophical metaphor to rigorous mathematics. The present dissertation undertakes that task, assembling a formal framework out of several powerful mathematical traditions:

· Operator Theory and Markov Processes. We treat the agent’s internal update as a Markov kernel acting on a space of interventional belief distributions. The central tool is the Dobrushin ergodicity coefficient (Dobrushin, 1956), which measures the contraction of a Markov operator with respect to the total variation norm. Recent generalizations of the Dobrushin coefficient to ordered Banach spaces provide convergence rates, uniqueness guarantees, and perturbation bounds—exactly the machinery needed to prove that $\Phi$ has a unique attractive fixed point.
· Information Theory and Compression. The compression step $\mathcal{C}$ is formulated as an Information Bottleneck (Tishby et al., 1999): the agent learns a latent representation $Z_t$ that minimizes $I(E_t; Z_t)$ while preserving enough information about future intervention outcomes $S_{t+1}$. This compels the agent to discard spurious correlations and retain only the causal signal. The theoretical limit of such compression is given by Kolmogorov complexity; recent work by Liu et al. (2025) shows that causal structures emerge naturally from compression across multiple environments.
· Causal Inference. To make the notion of “meaning” precise, we draw on Pearl’s structural causal models and the identifiability theory of Additive Noise Models (Peters et al., 2014). Semantic understanding is defined as Level‑2 grounding: a symbol $\sigma$ is understood if the agent possesses a map $\Gamma_2(\sigma) = P(\mathcal{S}\mid do(\sigma))$—the ability to predict the effect of intervening on the referent of $\sigma$.
· Embodied Cognition and World Models. The framework inherits from the embodied intelligence tradition (Harnad, 1990; Pfeifer & Bongard, 2006) the insistence that meaning arises from sensorimotor interaction, not passive exposure to symbols. It also connects to modern world‑model architectures (e.g., LeCun’s JEPA, DreamerV3) while sharpening the causal requirements that those architectures partially satisfy.

The dissertation proceeds in three main parts. Part I (Chapters 1‑2) sets up the formal environment, the agent architecture, and the complete metric space of interventional beliefs $(\mathcal{Z},d)$. Part II (Chapters 3‑5) contains the core mathematical results: the explicit computation of the Dobrushin coefficient for the Bayesian update kernel, the proof that $\Phi$ is a contraction iff the environment exhibits a positive minimum causal discernibility $\delta_{\min}>0$, the existence and uniqueness of the stable causal abstraction $z^*$, and the characterization of $z^*$ as a sufficient statistic for interventional prediction. We also prove perturbation bounds and derive sample complexity. Part III (Chapter 6‑7) translates the theory into an experimental protocol: a minimal 3D world, a causal agent architecture, and an interventional transfer metric that tests whether the agent has genuinely extracted causal laws rather than memorized surface patterns.

The central insight is that stable semantic grounding is a phase transition in the learning dynamics. When the world’s causal signal is too weak—when no intervention produces a discernible difference between at least two states—the loop fails to contract and meaning cannot be uniquely fixed. But as soon as that minimal discriminability is present, a unique stable abstraction emerges, and with it, genuine understanding. By proving this with the full rigor of Dobrushin’s contraction theory, the dissertation aims to place the foundations of grounded artificial intelligence on firm mathematical ground.

Part I — Mathematical Foundations

Chapter 1: The Problem

1.1 The Semantic Gap in Large Language Models

Contemporary large language models (LLMs) approximate the distribution

P_\theta(x_{t+1} \mid x_{\le t}),

where x_1,\dots,x_t is a sequence of tokens, by minimising cross‑entropy over vast corpora. The resulting fluency is remarkable, yet the model operates strictly at Pearl’s Level 1: associational reasoning. It may predict that the word “apple” co‑occurs with “fruit” and “cider”, but it cannot answer a single interventional question: “What happens if I drop the apple?”. Such a question demands Level 2 knowledge—conditional probabilities under deliberate interventions, P(S' \mid do(\sigma)).

This is the Symbol Grounding Problem (Harnad, 1990) recast in causal terms. Meaning that supports action and counterfactual thought requires more than statistical correlation; it requires an internal model of how one’s own actions transform the world. The central thesis of this dissertation is therefore:

\text{Understanding} \;=\; \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{E}),

where \mathcal{E} is the stream of embodied sensorimotor experience and \mathcal{C} is a causal compression operator—a map that extracts from interaction the minimal structure needed for interventional prediction.

The rest of Part I formalises the environment, the agent, and the space in which such understanding can be proved to converge.

