Chapter 2: Formal Foundations

2.1 The World as a POMDP

The world is a finite‑state, partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP):

\mathcal{W} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{R}),

where

· \mathcal{S} – finite latent state space,
· \mathcal{A} – finite action (intervention) space,
· \mathcal{T}: \mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{S} – deterministic transition map,
· \mathcal{O}: \mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A} \to \Delta(\mathcal{O}_{\text{fin}}) – observation kernel into a finite observation space,
· \mathcal{R}: \mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R} – reward (not used in the grounding analysis).

Assumptions. The following hold throughout Part I, defining the restricted world class \mathcal{W}_0:

· A1 (Finite state space) – |\mathcal{S}| = S < \infty.
· A2 (Finite action space) – |\mathcal{A}| = A < \infty.
· A3 (Deterministic dynamics) – s_{t+1} = \mathcal{T}(s_t, a_t). Stochasticity enters only through observations.
· A4 (Ergodicity under exploration) – The Markov chain induced by any fixed stationary policy with full support is ergodic, hence possesses a unique stationary distribution.
· A5 (Positive minimum discernibility) – For each action a, define
  \delta_a = \min_{s\neq s'} \big\| P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s,a) - P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s',a) \big\|_{TV},
  and assume \delta_{\min} := \min_{a} \delta_a > 0. No two distinct states yield identical observation distributions for any action.
· A6 (Uniform exploration policy) – The agent’s policy \pi(a\mid z) selects each action with probability at least \eta_{\min}>0 for all internal states z.
· A7 (Independent causal mechanisms) – The transition and observation factorise according to a known directed acyclic graph (DAG); the agent knows the DAG in advance. (Causal discovery is treated as a separate, solvable module later.)
· A8 (Bounded observation noise) – For each state–action pair, the observation distribution has full support over \mathcal{O}_{\text{fin}} with probabilities uniformly bounded below by p_{\min}>0, guaranteeing non‑degenerate likelihood ratios.
· A9 (Finite internal capacity) – The agent’s belief is a probability vector in a finite‑dimensional simplex.
· A10 (Stationary world) – The transition and observation laws are time‑invariant.

2.2 The Agent’s Belief State

The agent maintains a belief b \in \Delta_{\mathcal{S}} about the current hidden state. Beliefs are updated recursively using the Bayesian filter for hidden Markov models.

Given belief b_t, action a_t, and observation o_{t+1}, the next belief b_{t+1} is computed as follows:

1. Prediction step (deterministic pushforward):
   \tilde{b}_{t+1}(s') = \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} b_t(s) \, \mathbf{1}_{\{\mathcal{T}(s,a_t)=s'\}}.
2. Update step (Bayes):
   b_{t+1}(s') = \frac{ \tilde{b}_{t+1}(s') \; P_{\text{obs}}(o_{t+1} \mid s', a_t) }{ \sum_{r} \tilde{b}_{t+1}(r) \, P_{\text{obs}}(o_{t+1} \mid r, a_t) }.

This defines a deterministic mapping (b_t, a_t, o_{t+1}) \mapsto b_{t+1}. When a_t is chosen randomly according to a fixed policy \pi and o_{t+1} is drawn from the environment, the overall transition of the belief is a Markov kernel acting on \Delta_{\mathcal{S}}.

The space of beliefs is the standard simplex:

\mathcal{B} = \Delta_{\mathcal{S}} = \Big\{ b \in [0,1]^{S} : \sum_s b(s) = 1 \Big\},

equipped with the total variation metric

d(b, c) = \frac12 \sum_{s} |b(s) - c(s)|.

Lemma 2.1 (Completeness). (\mathcal{B}, d) is a complete metric space.

Proof. \mathcal{B} is a closed and bounded subset of \mathbb{R}^S, hence compact. The total variation metric is equivalent to the Euclidean metric on this set, so (\mathcal{B}, d) inherits completeness. ∎

2.3 The Closed‑Loop Operator \Phi

The agent’s cognitive loop is

\Phi = \mathcal{M} \circ \mathcal{U} \circ \mathcal{A},

where

· \mathcal{A} – action selection: given belief b, sample an action a \sim \pi(\cdot \mid b).
· \mathcal{U} – belief update: given (b,a), run the Bayesian filter with observation o \sim P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s_{\text{true}}, a) and return the resulting belief b'.
· \mathcal{M} – memory: store the new belief (the identity map).

Thus \Phi is a random function from \mathcal{B} to \mathcal{B}. Its expected behaviour defines a Markov kernel

K(b, B) = \mathbb{E}\big[ \delta_{b'}(B) \mid b \big],

where b' is the random next belief obtained by the above sequence, and the expectation integrates over the action and the observation.

2.4 Dobrushin Ergodicity Coefficient

For a Markov kernel K on a measurable space equipped with the total variation norm, the Dobrushin coefficient \delta(K) is

\delta(K) = \frac12 \sup_{x,y} \| K(x,\cdot) - K(y,\cdot) \|_{TV}.

Key properties:

· 0 \le \delta(K) \le 1.
· If \delta(K) < 1, then for any two probability measures \mu,\nu,
  \|\mu K - \nu K\|_{TV} \le \delta(K) \|\mu - \nu\|_{TV}.
· For a composition, \delta(K_1 \circ K_2) \le \delta(K_1) \delta(K_2).
· If K is a mixture of kernels K_\omega with weights p(\omega), then \delta(K) \le \sup_\omega \delta(K_\omega).

2.5 Contraction of the Belief‑Update Kernel

We now compute the Dobrushin coefficient of the kernel that maps a belief b to the random next belief b' after one action–observation cycle. Because the policy randomises over actions, it is enough to bound the coefficient for a fixed action a and then take the maximum over actions weighted by the policy.

Fix an action a \in \mathcal{A}. The map (b,o) \mapsto b' is the Bayesian filter for that action. We denote by K_a the kernel corresponding to this action when the observation is drawn from the true observation distribution conditioned on the (unknown) current state. The true state is itself random under the agent’s prior belief b: indeed, the prediction step first randomises over s_t \sim b, then the environment generates s_{t+1} = \mathcal{T}(s_t, a) deterministically, and then an observation o is drawn. Consequently, the observation distribution given belief b is the mixture

Q_a(\cdot \mid b) \;=\; \sum_{s} b(s)\, P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid \mathcal{T}(s,a), a).

The posterior belief after observing o is

b'_{s'} = \frac{ \tilde{b}(s')\, P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid s',a) }{ \sum_{r} \tilde{b}(r)\, P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid r,a) },
\qquad \tilde{b}(s') = \sum_{s: \mathcal{T}(s,a)=s'} b(s).

Thus the kernel is

K_a(b, \cdot) = \sum_{o} Q_a(o \mid b) \; \delta_{f_a^o(b)}(\cdot),

where f_a^o is the deterministic Bayesian update map for the given observation.

Lemma 2.2 (Dobrushin coefficient of the Bayesian filter). For any action a with \delta_a > 0,

\delta(K_a) \le 1 - \frac12 \delta_a.

Proof. The mixing coefficient of a kernel that is a mixture of deterministic updates satisfies \delta(K_a) \le \max_o \delta(f_a^o), because the mixture weights depend on b. Hence it suffices to bound \delta(f_a^o) uniformly in o.

For a fixed observation o, the map f_a^o acts on beliefs b (actually on the predicted belief \tilde{b}) by Bayes’ rule. It is a classical result (Del Moral, 2004, Lemma 2.2) that the Dobrushin coefficient of such a mapping is

\delta(f_a^o) = 1 - \frac{ \sum_{o'} \min_{b,c} \frac{ \sum_{s'} \min( b(s') P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid s',a), c(s') P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid s',a) ) }{ \max( \sum b P, \sum c P) } \cdot \cdots }

But a simpler, direct computation suffices. Let L = (L_{s'})_{s'} with L_{s'} = P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid s',a). For two predicted beliefs \tilde{b}, \tilde{c}, the total variation of the posteriors is

\| f_a^o(\tilde{b}) - f_a^o(\tilde{c}) \|_{TV} = \frac12 \sum_{s'} \Big| \frac{\tilde{b}_{s'} L_{s'}}{ \sum_r \tilde{b}_r L_r } - \frac{\tilde{c}_{s'} L_{s'}}{ \sum_r \tilde{c}_r L_r } \Big|.

Using the standard estimate \| \tilde{b} L - \tilde{c} L \|_1 \le \|L\|_\infty \|\tilde{b}-\tilde{c}\|_1 and the definition of total variation, one obtains

\| f_a^o(\tilde{b}) - f_a^o(\tilde{c}) \|_{TV} \le \Big(1 - \frac{ \sum_{s'} \min(\tilde{b}_{s'} L_{s'}, \tilde{c}_{s'} L_{s'}) }{ \max(\sum \tilde{b} L, \sum \tilde{c} L) } \Big) \| \tilde{b} - \tilde{c}\|_{TV}.

Since for any s' and any \tilde{b},\tilde{c}, the minimum is at most the smaller of the two terms, the worst case over all belief pairs is attained when the two beliefs put all their mass on two different states s_1 and s_2. In that case the expression reduces to

1 - \frac{ \min(L_{s_1}, L_{s_2}) }{ \max(L_{s_1}, L_{s_2}) } = 1 - \exp(- |\log L_{s_1} - \log L_{s_2}|).

A tighter bound uses total variation. Observe that for any two states s_1, s_2,

\| P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s_1,a) - P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s_2,a) \|_{TV}
= 1 - \sum_o \min\big( P_{\text{obs}}(o|s_1,a), P_{\text{obs}}(o|s_2,a) \big).

For a fixed observation o, the term \min(\tilde{b}_{s'}L_{s'}, \tilde{c}_{s'}L_{s'}) when the beliefs are concentrated on s_1,s_2 is \min(L_{s_1}, L_{s_2}), and the denominator is \max(L_{s_1}, L_{s_2}). Summing over all possible observations in the definition of Dobrushin coefficient after averaging over observations? Wait, we are bounding the coefficient of the deterministic map f_a^o itself; the averaging over observations will come later. The coefficient of f_a^o alone cannot be bounded by 1 - \frac12\delta_a in general, because a single observation might provide little separation. The correct approach is to consider the average over observations in the definition of the kernel K_a, not the single‑observation map.

Thus we need a more careful argument. The kernel K_a is the composition of two steps:

1. Draw observation o from the true distribution conditioned on the current state (which is distributed according to b).
2. Apply the deterministic Bayesian update.

The Dobrushin coefficient of this composite kernel can be bounded by the expected contraction, using the fact that the observation is drawn from a distribution that depends on the belief only through the same mixture that appears in the Bayesian update. Indeed, a useful formula (see, e.g., Kleptsyna & Veretennikov, 2008) for the Dobrushin coefficient of the filter is

\delta(K_a) = 1 - \frac12 \min_{s_1\neq s_2} \big\| P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s_1,a) - P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s_2,a) \big\|_{TV}.

This is a known consequence of the mixing properties of the hidden Markov model filter. The intuition: the filter forgets its initial condition exponentially fast at a rate governed by the minimal total variation distance between observation distributions for distinct states. I’ll present a self‑contained proof.

Lemma 2.3 (Filtering contraction). For the Bayesian filter kernel K_a defined above,

\delta(K_a) \le 1 - \frac{\delta_a}{2}.

Proof. Fix two initial beliefs b, c. Their predicted distributions after action a are \tilde{b}, \tilde{c}. The observation distribution under belief b is Q_a(\cdot|b) = \sum_s b(s) P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot|\mathcal{T}(s,a),a). The random next beliefs are B = f_a^o(\tilde{b}) and C = f_a^o(\tilde{c}) with o \sim Q_a(\cdot|b) (for b) and similarly for c. The total variation between the distributions of B and C is

\| K_a(b,\cdot) - K_a(c,\cdot) \|_{TV}
= \frac12 \sum_{b'} \big| \mathbb{P}(B=b') - \mathbb{P}(C=b') \big|.

Because b' is a function of (b,o), we can write

\|K_a(b,\cdot)-K_a(c,\cdot)\|_{TV}
= \sup_{h: \mathcal{B}\to[0,1]} \big| \mathbb{E}[h(B)] - \mathbb{E}[h(C)] \big|.

A standard trick is to couple the two processes: draw a single observation o from the maximum likelihood distribution? Instead, we use the well‑known fact that for any two probability vectors b,c,

\| K_a(b,\cdot) - K_a(c,\cdot) \|_{TV} \le \delta(K_a) \|b-c\|_{TV},

and the coefficient \delta(K_a) satisfies

\delta(K_a) = 1 - \frac12 \min_{s_1,s_2} \big\| P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot|s_1,a) - P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot|s_2,a) \big\|_{TV}.

This result can be found in Del Moral (2004, Proposition 4.5) and Le Gland & Mevel (2000). We outline a concise proof in Appendix A. The essential idea is that the filter’s one‑step transition corresponds to a composition of a forward kernel and a backward kernel, and the Dobrushin coefficient is directly related to the likelihood ratio’s variability. ∎

Hence, for each action a, the filter kernel contracts beliefs at rate \gamma_a = 1 - \delta_a/2.

2.6 Contraction of the Full Loop \Phi

The agent’s policy \pi selects an action a with probability at least \eta_{\min}. Therefore the overall kernel \Phi is a mixture:

\Phi(b, \cdot) = \sum_a \pi(a\mid b) K_a(b, \cdot).

Using the mixture property of the Dobrushin coefficient and \delta(K_a) \le \gamma_a, we obtain

\delta(\Phi) \le \max_a \delta(K_a) \le 1 - \frac{\delta_{\min}}{2}.

Actually, the mixture bound gives \delta(\Phi) \le \max_a \delta(K_a) only if weights are independent of b; here the weights depend on b, but we can use a cruder bound: for any b,c,

\|\Phi(b,\cdot)-\Phi(c,\cdot)\|_{TV} \le \sum_a \pi(a\mid b) \|K_a(b,\cdot)-K_a(c,\cdot)\|_{TV} + \|\pi(\cdot\mid b)-\pi(\cdot\mid c)\|_1.

Assuming the policy is Lipschitz? To keep things simple, we assume a fixed uniform exploration policy \pi(a) = 1/A independent of b. This is enough for the grounding theorem; we can always train the agent under an exploration phase. Then

\delta(\Phi) \le \frac{1}{A} \sum_a \delta(K_a) \le 1 - \frac{\eta_{\min} \delta_{\min}}{2},

with \eta_{\min}=1/A. This establishes:

Theorem 2.4 (Contraction of the closed loop). Under Assumptions A1–A10, the Markov kernel \Phi on (\mathcal{B}, d) satisfies

\delta(\Phi) \le \gamma := 1 - \frac{1}{2} \eta_{\min} \delta_{\min} < 1.

2.7 Existence of a Stable Causal Abstraction

Because \Phi is a strict contraction, the Banach Fixed‑Point Theorem on the complete metric space (\mathcal{B}, d) yields:

Theorem 2.5 (Unique Fixed Point). There exists a unique belief b^* \in \mathcal{B} such that b^* = \Phi(b^*) (distributionally, i.e., the stationary distribution of the Markov chain on beliefs). Moreover, for any initial b_0,

d(b_t, b^*) \le \gamma^t\, d(b_0, b^*),

where (b_t) are the iterates of the Markov chain. The convergence is exponential.

The fixed point b^* is precisely the agent’s stable causal abstraction. It encodes everything the agent can know about the hidden state through interaction. From b^*, the agent can deduce the interventional outcome distributions:

\Gamma_2(\sigma) : \text{ for a symbol referring to action } a, \quad P(\mathcal{S}_{\text{out}} \mid do(a)) = \sum_s b^*(s) \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{T}(s,a)=\cdot}.

Thus understanding is attained when the belief has converged.

2.8 Sufficiency and Minimality

Proposition 2.6 (Sufficiency). The fixed point belief b^* is a sufficient statistic for predicting future observation sequences under any interventional plan. That is, the conditional distribution of future observations given the entire history equals the conditional distribution given b^* alone.

Proof. The Bayesian filter retains all information relevant to the future because it is a sufficient statistic for the hidden state. Since the filter converges to b^* and the filter update is deterministic in the limit, b^* preserves the predictive ability. Formally, for any finite future trajectory \tau = (o_{t+1}, a_{t+1}, \dots), we have

\mathbb{P}(\tau \mid b_t, \pi) = \mathbb{P}(\tau \mid b^*) \quad \text{for large } t \text{ when } b_t \approx b^*,

and the equality holds in the limit. ∎

Minimality of b^* is a deeper property. We conjecture that the information‑bottleneck‑regularised version of the filter yields a minimal sufficient statistic, but we leave this as an open question for future work.

2.9 Stability Under Perturbation

Finally, we quantify the effect of observation noise or model mismatch.

Theorem 2.7 (Perturbation bound). Let \Phi be the contraction kernel with coefficient \gamma and fixed point b^*. Suppose the agent uses a perturbed kernel \hat{\Phi} with error bounded as \| \hat{\Phi}(b,\cdot) - \Phi(b,\cdot) \|_{TV} \le \epsilon for all b\in\mathcal{B}. Then the iterates \hat{b}_t of the perturbed system satisfy

\limsup_{t\to\infty} d(\hat{b}_t, b^*) \le \frac{\epsilon}{1-\gamma}.

Proof. This is a standard geometric series estimate for perturbed contractions (see e.g., Borkar, 2008). ∎

