Chapter 13: Experimental Hypotheses

We now state the testable predictions derived from the theory. All hypotheses are pre‑registered.

Hypothesis 1 (Contraction and Convergence).
For the default environment with \Delta \approx 0.2, the agent’s belief (as measured by the distance to the true fixed point) will decrease exponentially with the number of iterations, and the mean distance will drop below 0.05 within T_0 = 5000 interactions (as predicted by the sample complexity bound). The convergence rate will closely match the theoretical contraction coefficient \gamma = 1 - \Delta/2.

Hypothesis 2 (Transfer of Causal Abstraction).
The grounded agent will achieve a mean ITS > 0.7 on the novel object set, while the Level‑1 baseline will score close to 0. This demonstrates that the agent has extracted causal laws rather than memorised perceptual patterns.

Hypothesis 3 (Effect of Reduced Discriminability).
When we increase the observation noise to reduce \Delta from 0.2 to 0.05 (while keeping all other parameters fixed), the contraction coefficient \gamma approaches 1, and the required number of interactions to reach the target accuracy grows as 1/\Delta^2. In our fixed budget of 10^5 interactions, the agent will fail to converge (i.e., final TV distance > 0.05) in the low‑\Delta condition, while it converges easily in the high‑\Delta condition. This failure is due to statistical infeasibility, not a breakdown of the contraction property.

Hypothesis 4 (Robustness to Perturbation).
If we artificially perturb the belief after each update by adding a bounded noise vector (simulating model mismatch), the final steady‑state error will be bounded by \epsilon/(1-\gamma) as per Theorem 2.7. We will test this with \epsilon = 0.01 and verify the bound.

