Appendix A: Full Dobrushin Contraction Proof for the Belief Filter

This appendix provides a self‑contained proof of Lemma 2.3, establishing that the Bayesian update kernel for a single action a satisfies

\delta(K_a) = 1 - \frac12\,\delta_a,\qquad \delta_a = \min_{s\neq s'} \|P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot\mid s,a) - P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot\mid s',a)\|_{TV}.

We then combine actions to obtain the contraction coefficient of the full loop \Phi.

A.1 Setting

Fix an action a. The agent’s belief space is \mathcal{B} = \Delta_{\mathcal{S}}, the simplex over the finite state space \mathcal{S}. The kernel K_a maps a belief b \in \mathcal{B} to the distribution over the next belief B' obtained after (i) the deterministic state transition s' = \mathcal{T}(s,a), and (ii) observing o drawn from P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot \mid s', a). Formally,

K_a(b, \cdot) = \sum_{o \in \mathcal{O}} \Bigl( \sum_{s} b(s) \, P_{\text{obs}}\!(o \mid \mathcal{T}(s,a), a) \Bigr) \; \delta_{f_a^o(b)}(\cdot),

where f_a^o is the Bayes‑posterior map:

f_a^o(b)(s') = \frac{ \tilde{b}(s') \, P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid s', a) }{ \sum_{r} \tilde{b}(r) \, P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid r, a) }, \qquad
\tilde{b}(s') = \sum_{s:\mathcal{T}(s,a)=s'} b(s).

The kernel K_a acts on the complete metric space (\mathcal{B},d) with d(b,c)=\frac12\|b-c\|_1.

A.2 Forward kernel decomposition

Define the forward kernel F_a : \mathcal{B} \to \Delta_{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{O}} by

F_a(b, (s',o)) = \tilde{b}(s') \, P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid s', a).

This kernel sends a belief b to the joint distribution of the (predicted) next state s' and the observation o. A backward (Bayesian) kernel B_a : \Delta_{\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{O}} \to \Delta_{\mathcal{S}} deterministically produces the posterior:

B_a(\mu, b') = \mathbf{1}_{\{b' = \text{Bayes}(\mu)\}}.

Then K_a = B_a \circ F_a. Because B_a is deterministic, \delta(B_a)=1 (non‑expansive). Consequently, \delta(K_a) \le \delta(F_a); actually for the product we have \delta(K_a) \le \delta(F_a)\delta(B_a) = \delta(F_a). Conversely, since F_a is the observation‑conditioned push‑forward, one can show \delta(F_a) \le \delta(K_a). Therefore the contraction coefficients are equal: \delta(K_a) = \delta(F_a). We thus analyse the simpler forward kernel.

A.3 Total variation distance for the forward kernel

For two beliefs b,c,

\|F_a(b,\cdot)-F_a(c,\cdot)\|_{TV}
= \frac12 \sum_{s',o} \Bigl| \sum_{s} (b(s)-c(s)) \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{T}(s,a)=s'} \, P_{\text{obs}}(o \mid s', a) \Bigr|.

Let us define, for each state pair (s_1,s_2), the observation overlap

\kappa(s_1,s_2) = \sum_o \min\!\bigl(P_{\text{obs}}(o\mid s_1,a),\, P_{\text{obs}}(o\mid s_2,a)\bigr).

