Appendix D: Counterexample for \Delta = 0

To illustrate the necessity of \Delta > 0 for a unique grounded abstraction, we construct a minimal environment where two states are completely indistinguishable under all actions.

World. \mathcal{S} = \{0,1\}, \mathcal{A} = \{a_1\}. Transition: \mathcal{T}(0,a_1) = 0, \mathcal{T}(1,a_1) = 0 (both map to state 0). Observation: P_{\text{obs}}(\cdot|0,a_1) is a fixed distribution, and the same for the next state. So the observation distribution after any action is identical irrespective of the starting state. Thus \Delta = 0.

Belief update. For any initial belief b = (p,1-p), after action a_1 the agent observes o and computes posterior: the predicted belief is deterministic: both states go to 0, so \tilde{b} = (1,0). Then Bayes update with observation o from distribution Q independent of the original state gives posterior (1,0) unchanged. Thus the belief remains (1,0) forever, regardless of initial belief. Therefore every initial belief is a fixed point! The system has a continuum of fixed points, no unique stable abstraction. Grounding fails.

Thus, \Delta=0 indeed prevents the emergence of a unique semantic understanding. This completes the proof of the necessity direction.

